-### C11. Process definitions and the frozen library
-
-**C11-C14 are an interrupted candidate proposal.** Their record formats, role
-mapping and process-owned tool menus are not settled design requirements. The
-following text preserves the proposal for review; it is not permission to resume
-coding. Parent construction and its prompts remain under discussion.
-
-The candidate followed inspection of the complete
-C7 registry/composer, shared Grain/tree/Context paths, literature binding, source
-ports, and commission/worker reconstruction. The common `Episode` already owns
-iteration. Library-declared process names fit its existing role-indexed schemas,
-credit kinds, tree edges and full-path identities. A single undifferentiated
-role would require replacing those contracts and maintaining another process
-graph. Use one common `LibraryEpisodes` binding with data-declared roles instead.
-Declaring type metadata does not instantiate children or prescribe an itinerary.
-
-One process ID equals its role name in this version. IDs are nonempty strings
-matching `[a-z][a-z0-9_]*`. No role alias or new Episode subclass is needed when
-adding a process using existing mechanics. Compact metadata can be loaded for
-registration; full definitions and guidance remain separately addressable.
-
-All records below are closed, finite JSON. Digests use the existing
-`method_loop.durable.content_digest` canonical semantics. Unknown/missing values
-are not supplied by inference. New pure module `process_records.py` exports:
-
-```python
-process_digest(value) -> str  # full SHA-256, existing canonical semantics
-validate_definition(value) -> dict  # plain independent validated copy
-validate_process_ref(value) -> dict
-validate_process_binding(value) -> dict
-```
-
-`ProcessDefinitionV1` has exactly these required fields:
-
-```text
-version: "episode_process_definition_v1"
-process_id, title, summary: nonempty strings
-tags: unique nonempty strings[]
-entry: bool
-credit_kind: "scientific" | "operational" | "monitoring"
-decision_question: nonempty string identifying the recurring decision
-instructions: nonempty process guidance string
-input_schema: JSON Schema object for the parent's selected input values
-assignment_schema: JSON Schema object for the prepared child assignment
-common_actions: unique values from tool,reason,child,wait,redeliver_report
-common_tools: unique exact runtime-tool names (enumerated below)
-children: unique process_id[] of permitted subprocesses
-operations: [{name: nonempty actual operation name,
-              arguments_schema: JSON Schema object | null}]
-mechanics: {id: nonempty string, configuration: finite JSON object}
-guidance_sections: [{section_id: nonempty string, title: nonempty string,
-                     path: relative UTF-8 guidance-file path}]
-```
-
-Input/assignment schemas describe object inputs with explicit properties and a
-closed root. Argument constraints may be partial schemas: they are intersected
-with actual executable contracts, never substituted for them. Schemas must be
-valid; external `$ref` dependencies are not part of this snapshot format. Process
-IDs, operation names and section IDs are unique in their respective lists.
-Definitions describe recurring decisions and alternatives, not steps, timers,
-retry rules or child counts. The parent still supplies its own return form.
-
-Allowed `common_tools` are `process.search`, `process.entry`, `process.read`,
-`knowledge.search`, `knowledge.entry`, `knowledge.read`, `episode.history`,
-`episode.credit`, `episode.annotation_score`, `episode.estimate_wait`, and
-`episode.monitoring_information`. Actual host support is still required. These
-are inspection/retrieval operations, distinct from primitive domain operations
-in `operations`. The common runtime must not append excluded tools afterward.
-
-The source directory has `index.json` exactly:
-
-```text
-{version:"episode_process_library_v1", definition_files:[relative JSON path]}
-```
-
-Each file contains one ProcessDefinitionV1. Every declared child must resolve in
-that same library. The snapshot manifest stores each definition's relative path,
-canonical digest and compact header, and each guidance file's byte digest and
-line count. `ProcessHeaderV1` is exactly `{process_id,title,summary,tags,entry,
-credit_kind,children,mechanics_id,definition_sha256}`. A library identity is a
-content digest of this manifest's definitions and guidance information, excluding
-its own identity field. The public snapshot descriptor is exactly
-`{version:"episode_process_library_snapshot_v1",library_id,manifest_sha256}`.
-Its physical location is the caller's run directory, not an executable import.
-
-`ProcessRefV1` is exactly `{version:"episode_process_reference_v1",library_id,
-process_id,definition_sha256}`. Opening a saved snapshot never refreezes a newer
-source. Definition and guidance reads verify saved hashes. Missing/changed bytes
-are specific failures, never permission to select another process. The library
-does not retain all definition/manual bodies in active state.
-
-New `process_library.py` exports:
-
-```python
-ProcessLibrary.freeze(directory, *, source) -> ProcessLibrary
-ProcessLibrary(directory)  # reopen existing manifest; no implicit freeze
-.as_record() -> snapshot descriptor above
-.headers() -> tuple[ProcessHeaderV1, ...]
-.reference(process_id) -> ProcessRefV1
-.definition(process_id) -> ProcessDefinitionV1
-.search(*, query, offset, limit) -> {
-    library: snapshot descriptor, entries: [ProcessHeaderV1],
-    offset, limit, next_offset: int|null}
-.entry(process_id) -> {process_ref, definition,
-    guidance_index:[{section_id,title,sha256,line_count}]}
-.read(*, process_id, section_id, offset, limit) -> {
-    process_ref,section_id,sha256,offset,text,total_lines,next_offset:int|null}
-.execute(name, arguments) -> existing ToolOutcome
-catalog() -> list[ToolSpec]  # process.search/entry/read only
-```
-
-Search uses literal case-insensitive metadata terms and explicit paging; it
-does not assess scientific relevance. Empty search pages metadata. Read offsets
-count zero-based UTF-8 text lines, not scientific source bytes. Search/read
-limits are positive integers; offsets are nonnegative. Retrieval outcomes have
-no scientific records, credit, native submissions or fabricated measurements.
-Structured failures identify missing process/section, invalid operands, malformed
-library or changed snapshot. A failed retrieval does not become completed work.
-
-### C12. Common template and mechanical adapters
-
-New `process_mechanics.py` defines the runtime-checkable `ProcessMechanics` port
-and `PlainProcessMechanics(services)`. The latter has ID `plain`, empty definition
-configuration, ordinary service execution/resolution, and empty additional
-context/facts/receipt hooks. It supplies no question, instructions, child menu,
-tool menu or credit formula. It is reusable for new scientific process data.
-
-Every mechanics object implements these exact ports. `definition` is the selected
-ProcessDefinitionV1; `context` is the actual C7 BindingContext, never role-aliased.
-
-```python
-mechanics_id: str
-as_record() -> nonempty finite JSON object  # actual implementation/resources
-validate_definition(definition) -> None  # its mechanical configuration only
-prepare_assignment(parent, definition, inputs, scope, operation_key,
-                   *, parent_definition=None) -> dict
-validate_assignment(context, definition) -> None
-decision_context(context, definition, current_view) -> dict
-catalog(context, definition) -> list[ToolSpec]  # actual bound capabilities
-eligible_children(context, definition, declared_roles, caller_capabilities)
-    -> tuple[str, ...]  # only mechanical eligibility; never expand the set
-execute(context, definition, tool_name, arguments, operation_key) -> ToolOutcome
-fact_sources(definition) -> mapping[str, FactDefinition]
-facts(context, definition, completion_snapshot) -> dict
-resolve(context, definition, reference, namespace) -> bool
-validate_report(context, definition, form, original_schema) -> None
-initial_references(context, definition) -> list[str]
-received_form_references(context, form, schema, operation_key) -> list[str]
-```
-
-Execute may be async. Reference hooks have the existing M1 semantics; the
-received-form hook can be called for another receiver role and must not require
-that receiver to belong to this mechanics object. Plain/source hooks return [].
-Preparation is selected mechanical work/validation, not a process chooser; run
-it once. It cannot rewrite goal, scope, form or caller allocations. Unknown
-mechanics need an actual caller-supplied object; a library string cannot import
-or invent an executable. Non-scientific credit remains explicitly caller bound.
-
-New `library_episodes.py` exports:
-
-```python
-LibraryEpisodes(services, *, library, mechanics)
-# mechanics is Mapping[id, actual ProcessMechanics object]
-# Implements the complete existing EpisodeBinding protocol.
-.prepare_invocation(parent, role, inputs, scope, operation_key, *, invocation)
-    -> {assignment:dict, process_binding:ProcessBindingV1}
-.prepare_root(role, inputs, scope, operation_key, *, invocation)
-    -> {assignment:dict, process_binding:ProcessBindingV1}
-.validate_context(context) -> None
-.common_tool_names(context) -> tuple[str, ...]
-.initial_references(context) -> list[str]
-.received_form_references(context, form, schema, operation_key) -> list[str]
-```
-
-`invocation` is exactly `{objective, purpose, report_schema, initial_report}`;
-purpose/schema are null for a root. Scope is passed separately. The mother uses
-the selected input schema, calls the selected mechanics preparation once, and
-validates the prepared assignment schema. The old C7 `prepare_assignment` remains
-implemented for protocol completeness; the new host uses prepare_invocation
-once rather than calling both. No loop/model/child execution belongs here.
-
-For compatibility with the existing host's child-selection consumer, C7
-`assignment_schema(role)` returns definition.input_schema, the parent's selectable
-operands. The separately named definition.assignment_schema validates the
-prepared assignment internally. This mapping is intentional; callers must not
-offer the prepared-only fields as parent-selected inputs. prepare_assignment
-returns only the prepared assignment; prepare_invocation additionally fills C13.
-prepare_root calls mechanics preparation once with parent=None and
-parent_definition=None, and the explicit operation_key. The host supplies
-`<run_id>:root:process` for root construction and reuses saved fills on resume.
-
-Mother initial_references returns [] outside its owned roles; otherwise it
-validates the selected fill and delegates to its mechanics. received_form_references
-aggregates every distinct bound mechanics object once, including for receivers
-outside the mother's roles, and deduplicates returned references. These are the
-existing optional registry hooks, required on this mother even though C7's base
-protocol does not require them. They add no selected action or observation.
-
-Roles, entry roles, credit kinds and child edges come from headers. Instructions
-come from the selected definition's recurring question and guidance, not the old
-role classes. Catalog is the intersection of `operations` with mechanics' actual
-capabilities; argument schema is the actual schema, or `allOf:[actual,constraint]`
-when a process supplies constraints. Validate that same effective schema before
-selected execution, so data cannot broaden a primitive contract. Child choices
-are the declared data edges filtered only for actual mechanical eligibility.
-
-The decision context contains `episode_process:{process_ref,decision_question,
-assignment,permitted_children,permitted_operations,guidance_index}` plus the
-mechanics' actual context. It contains only this selected process, not every
-process definition. Earlier outcomes and original admitted forms still arrive
-through the ordinary source. Scientific credit delegates to scientific_credit;
-other kinds delegate to bound_role_credit. Parent forms and recorded-fact
-validation use the existing protocol. Shared facts are not replaced by a new
-library-owned mandatory report.
-
-The binding descriptor is exactly:
-
-```text
-{version:"library_episode_binding_v1", binding_id:"library_episodes",
- implementation_sha256: actual template/mechanics-port Python code digest,
- configuration:{library: C11 snapshot descriptor,
-                mechanics:[{id,definition:actual mechanics.as_record()}]}}
